.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
reproctl setup
Bootstrap the entire environment.
.TP
reproctl start api\-server workspace
Start api\-server and workspace from main checkout.
.TP
reproctl stop \-\-all
Tear down all services and Tilt.
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
Shell completions can be generated via the \f[B]completion\f[R]
subcommand:
.IP
.EX
reproctl completion bash
reproctl completion zsh
reproctl completion fish
.EE
.PP
See \f[B]reproctl\-completion\f[R](1) for installation instructions.
.PP
Completions are also available as static files in
\f[B]scripts/completions/\f[R] for direct sourcing via \f[B].envrc\f[R]
or shell profiles.
.SH INTERACTIVE PICKER
Commands that accept a service name (\f[B]start\f[R], \f[B]stop\f[R],
\f[B]restart\f[R], \f[B]logs\f[R]) support a \f[B]\-\-pick\f[R] /
\f[B]\-p\f[R] flag to interactively select from available services.
If \f[B]fzf\f[R] is installed, it is used for fuzzy selection; otherwise
a numbered prompt is shown.
.PP
For \f[B]worktree attach\f[R] and \f[B]worktree remove\f[R], omitting
the branch argument triggers the picker automatically when stdin is a
terminal.
.SH EXIT CODES
.TP
\f[B]0\f[R]
Success \(em the operation completed as expected.
.TP
\f[B]1\f[R]
Error \(em the operation failed (bad input, runtime failure, missing
prerequisite).
.TP
\f[B]2\f[R]
User cancelled \(em an interactive prompt was cancelled (Ctrl\-C in a
picker, empty input).
.PP
Status commands (\f[B]status\f[R], \f[B]cluster status\f[R], \f[B]db
status\f[R], \f[B]context\f[R]) return 0 when they successfully produce
output, even if the thing being checked is unhealthy.
Health\-assertion commands (\f[B]checkhealth\f[R], \f[B]doctor\f[R])
return 1 when errors are found.
.PP
See \f[B]reproctl\-exit\-codes\f[R](1) for the full exit code
conventions.
